--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 1514 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I won’t repeat or store your SSN — you don’t need to share that here. Quick question first: when you say “expiry date,” do you mean your passport’s expiry or the visa’s expiry?

General guidance (most common rules)
- Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least 6 months beyond your intended date of entry or beyond the end of your planned stay. If your passport expires within that 6‑month window, that’s often considered “too close.”
- Some countries have different requirements (e.g., Schengen usually requires at least 3 months beyond departure; others explicitly require 6 months). Some countries require validity for the full visa duration.
- Airlines can refuse boarding if your passport doesn’t meet the destination’s entry rules, so don’t rely only on getting a visa — check both the embassy and your airline.
- If your passport would be invalid under the destination’s rule, the safe action is to renew your passport before applying or traveling. Expedited renewals are available in many countries.

What I can do next
- If you tell me the destination country and your passport expiry month/year (no SSN or other sensitive numbers), and the intended travel or visa start/end dates, I’ll check the exact entry/visa validity requirement for that country and tell you whether it’s likely to be a problem and what to do.
- Or I can draft a short message you can send to the embassy/consulate asking whether your passport is acceptable.

Which country and what passport expiry / travel dates?

